      "author": {
        "type": "character",
        "characterId": "chr_seedVictor"
      },
      "content": "Three points. One: \"nationwide\" need not mean \"identical\". The motion is about the default standard week, as we once did with the weekend.[1] Two: law already handles continuous-coverage sectors through shift rules.[2] Three: the burden of proof is on the status quo, a five-day week that was itself a policy choice, not a law of nature.",
      "status": "complete",
      "createdAt": "2026-10-01T10:03:05.701Z",
      "emotion": "happy",
      "emotionSource": "llm",
      "usage": {
        "tokensIn": 2655,
        "tokensCached": 1783,
        "tokensOut": 84,
        "costUsd": 0.000187,
        "energySpent": 2,
        "firstTokenMs": 1294,
        "totalMs": 3599
      },
      "citations": [
        {
          "n": 1,
          "sourceId": "kno_seedVictor1",
          "title": "Moot court notes",
          "type": "text",
          "chunkId": "kch_victorMoot02",
          "locator": "¶ 2",
          "quote": "Historical anchor: the Meridian Working Hours Act 1931 made the two-day weekend the default. It gave agriculture and shipping a ten-year transition and exempted continuous services.",
          "score": 0.86
        },
        {
          "n": 2,
          "sourceId": "kno_seedVictor1",
          "title": "Moot court notes",
          "type": "text",
          "chunkId": "kch_victorMoot03",
          "locator": "¶ 3",
          "quote": "Continuous-coverage sectors already run on shift rules under Part IV of the Working Time Code: rest periods, a cap on consecutive shifts, and averaging over a reference period. A shorter standard week sits inside that structure.",
          "score": 0.79
        }
      ],
